1. A method for binaural synthesis using a wearable audio device, the method comprising:

receiving, by one or more processors, an audio input signal representing audio content to be rendered to a listener;

determining, by the one or more processors, spatial rendering information for the audio input signal, the spatial rendering information corresponding to at least one virtual sound source location relative to a head of the listener;

generating, based at least in part on the spatial rendering information, a plurality of ear-specific drive signals for a plurality of loudspeakers of the wearable audio device, the plurality of loudspeakers comprising at least four loudspeakers positioned around both ears of the listener, including at least two loudspeakers associated with a left ear and at least two loudspeakers associated with a right ear;

processing the audio input signal to produce frequency-dependent signal components including high-frequency content within a range from 4 kHz to 12 kHz;

distributing the high-frequency content within the range from 4 kHz to 12 kHz across multiple loudspeakers associated with each respective ear, such that different portions of the high-frequency content are radiated from different positions around the respective ear; and

outputting the plurality of ear-specific drive signals through the plurality of loudspeakers to cause acoustic interaction with pinnae of the listener, thereby producing natural pinna cues for binaural perception of the at least one virtual sound source location.

1. A method for reproducing one or more virtual sound sources for a listener, the method comprising:

receiving one or more input audio signals corresponding to the one or more virtual sound sources;

for each virtual sound source, generating binaural audio signals comprising a left-ear signal and a right-ear signal by processing the corresponding input audio signal according to spatial information associated with the virtual sound source;

separating at least one of the left-ear signal or the right-ear signal into a high-frequency signal component and a remaining signal component;

for each ear of the listener, providing a plurality of loudspeakers positioned to reproduce sound toward the respective ear, the plurality of loudspeakers comprising at least first and second loudspeakers associated with the respective ear;

routing the high-frequency signal component for the respective ear to at least two of the plurality of loudspeakers associated with the respective ear; and

driving the plurality of loudspeakers to reproduce the binaural audio signals such that the one or more virtual sound sources are perceived by the listener at virtual positions.
